  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1204 PM CDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nCooler temperatures and widespread storm chances prevail through \nFriday before a warm and dry weekend. The upper trough mentioned in \nthe short term discussion will continue to slowly get absorbed into \nnortherly flow. Southwesterly flow aloft will prevail much of Friday \nbefore shifting to the west late Friday through early Saturday as \nthe deamplifying trough swings across the Central Plains and the \nupper ridging gradually shifts south. Subtropical moisture will \ncontinue to pump into the region through most of the day Friday. \nWith plenty of moisture and upper forcing from passing shortwaves, \nthere is potential for thunderstorms all day. NBM is very ambitious \nwith PoPs for Friday with some areas above 90 percent. With how the \npast couple of days have panned out, this forecaster is not as \nconfident, therefore have lowered PoPs to likely chances. \nThunderstorms on Friday are expected to remain sub-severe, however \nsome gusty winds cannot be ruled out. The main threat will be heavy \nrainfall that could lead to flooding with PWATs above 1.5\". \nProlonged precipitation and cloudy skies will keep temperatures in \nthe mid 70s across the far southwestern Texas Panhandle to lower 90s \nacross the Southern Rolling Plains. \n\nConditions will dry out and temperatures warm up over the weekend. \nThe upper ridging is expected to amplify across the forecast area. \nSubsidence below the region will keep precipitation chances near \nzero. Height and thickness increases will warm temperatures back \ninto the 80s to upper 90s through Monday. As the ridging sits \noverhead through the weekend, an upper trough will amplify down the \nwest coast and move onshore off the coast of California sometime \nearly next week. The upper trough is expected to swing across the \nRockies towards central CONUS effectively pushing the upper ridging \nto the east, however models are in disagreement on timing. ECMWF has \na faster progression with the trough reaching our region by Tuesday \nwhile GFS has a slower progression with the trough impacting the \nregion by mid-week. Either progression will bring cooler \ntemperatures and storm potential next week.",
